  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through Sunday)\nIssued at 109 PM CDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nWednesday is expected to be warmer and drier with highs in the upper \n70s to low 80s for much of the region. The pattern shifts on \nThursday as an upper level disturbance moves into west and central \nTexas from Mexico. Medium range guidance continues to show the \nhighest rain chances (40-70% with some areas over 80%) stretching \nfrom Big Bend and into the eastern Permian Basin during the day on \nThursday and into Thursday evening. Similar to the last few \nwidespread rain events in the region, some uncertainty remains \nwith just how much rain many areas will receive. Generally, areas\nwest of a line from Midland/Odessa to Fort Stockton will see less\nrain. Temperatures move near or just below normal in the low to \nmid 70s on Friday thanks to the clouds and rain in addition to \neasterly flow that will keep conditions slightly cooler. \n\nAs the aforementioned low moves out of the area, another weak \ndisturbance moves in from the west, but models continue to be \nbearish on rain chances with this system on Halloween and on \nNovember 1st. Beyond this coming weekend, rain chances dip further \nand temperatures stay just above normal in the mid to upper 70s. \n\n-Stickney",
